## 解答

**答案：** $\alpha \in \left(-\infty,\,-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)$.

---

**Setup.** Define $g(t)=t^{2}-\ln t$ on $\bigl[\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},\,+\infty\bigr)$. Then $g'(t)=2t-\tfrac{1}{t}>0$ on $\bigl(\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},+\infty\bigr)$, so $g$ is strictly increasing there, with minimum $g\!\bigl(\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}\bigr)=\tfrac{1+\ln 2}{2}$. The relation $g(f(x))=x$ with $f(x)\ge \tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}$ defines $f$ uniquely and strictly increasing on $\bigl[\tfrac{1+\ln 2}{2},+\infty\bigr)$. In particular $f(k)$ is well defined for every integer $k\ge 1$.

**Step 1 — $f(k)>\sqrt{k}$ (positivity of terms).** For $k\ge 1$,
$$g(\sqrt{k})=k-\tfrac12\ln k<k=g(f(k)).$$
Both $\sqrt{k}$ and $f(k)$ lie in $\bigl[\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2},+\infty\bigr)$ where $g$ is strictly increasing, hence $f(k)>\sqrt{k}$. So every term $k^{\alpha}(f(k)-\sqrt{k})$ is positive, and limit comparison is applicable.

**Step 2 — Asymptotic expansion of $f(x)-\sqrt{x}$.** Write $f(x)=\sqrt{x}\,h(x)$ with $h(x)\to 1$. Substituting into $f^{2}-\ln f=x$:
$$x\,h^{2}-\tfrac12\ln x-\ln h=x \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x(h^{2}-1)=\tfrac12\ln x+\ln h.$$
Set $u=h-1\to 0$; then $h^{2}-1=2u+u^{2}$ and $\ln h=u-\tfrac{u^{2}}{2}+O(u^{3})$, giving
$$2xu+xu^{2}=\tfrac12\ln x+u-\tfrac{u^{2}}{2}+O(u^{3}),$$
i.e.
$$u=\frac{\tfrac12\ln x-\tfrac{u^{2}}{2}-xu^{2}+O(u^{3})}{2x-1}.$$
Since $xu^{2}=O\!\bigl(\tfrac{(\ln x)^{2}}{x}\bigr)=o(\ln x)$, the dominant balance gives
$$u\sim\frac{\ln x}{4x},\qquad\text{hence}\qquad f(x)-\sqrt{x}=\sqrt{x}\,u\sim\frac{\ln x}{4\sqrt{x}}.$$
A standard bootstrap (re-substituting this leading term) confirms the next correction is $O\!\bigl(\tfrac{(\ln x)^{2}}{x^{3/2}}\bigr)$, so the limit is exact:
$$\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{f(x)-\sqrt{x}}{\dfrac{\ln x}{4\sqrt{x}}}=1.$$

**Step 3 — Limit comparison.** By Step 2,
$$k^{\alpha}\bigl(f(k)-\sqrt{k}\bigr)\sim \frac{1}{4}\,k^{\alpha-\frac12}\ln k\qquad(k\to\infty).$$
All terms are positive (Step 1), so by the limit comparison test the series $\sum_{k\ge 1}k^{\alpha}(f(k)-\sqrt{k})$ converges if and only if
$$\sum_{k\ge 1}k^{\alpha-\frac12}\ln k\quad\text{converges}.$$

**Step 4 — Convergence of $\sum k^{\beta}\ln k$.** By the integral test, this converges iff $\displaystyle\int^{\infty}x^{\beta}\ln x\,dx$ converges. For $\beta\ne -1$,
$$\int x^{\beta}\ln x\,dx=\frac{x^{\beta+1}}{\beta+1}\!\left(\ln x-\frac{1}{\beta+1}\right),$$
which has a finite limit at $+\infty$ iff $\beta+1<0$, i.e. $\beta<-1$. For $\beta=-1$, $\int \tfrac{\ln x}{x}\,dx=\tfrac{(\ln x)^{2}}{2}\to+\infty$, so it diverges. Therefore $\sum k^{\beta}\ln k$ converges $\iff \beta<-1$.

**Conclusion.** With $\beta=\alpha-\tfrac12$, convergence requires
$$\alpha-\tfrac12<-1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \alpha<-\tfrac12.$$

$$\boxed{\;\alpha\in\Bigl(-\infty,\,-\tfrac12\Bigr)\;}$$

